1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,252
Two teams must build the same shop, CloudCart.

2
00:00:03,802 --> 00:00:06,746
Team A plans everything first, then builds, then ships.

3
00:00:07,296 --> 00:00:10,539
Team B builds a small version, shows it, and improves it.

4
00:00:11,089 --> 00:00:14,097
Both teams follow the software development life cycle.

5
00:00:14,647 --> 00:00:17,740
But they arrange the stages in time very differently.

6
00:00:18,290 --> 00:00:21,789
Those arrangements have names, and knowing them is today's goal.

7
00:00:22,339 --> 00:00:27,096
By the end, you will tell Waterfall, Agile, Scrum, Kanban, and DevOps apart.

8
00:00:29,146 --> 00:00:30,597
Start with one distinction.

9
00:00:31,147 --> 00:00:36,949
The life cycle lists the stages: plan, design, build, test, release, deploy, and operate.

10
00:00:37,499 --> 00:00:41,915
Waterfall and Agile describe how those stages are arranged over time.

11
00:00:42,465 --> 00:00:45,815
Scrum and Kanban are ways of organizing the daily work.

12
00:00:46,365 --> 00:00:51,634
And DevOps extends the cycle, so delivery and operations are part of the team's job.

13
00:00:52,184 --> 00:00:54,253
Each level answers a different question.

14
00:00:56,303 --> 00:00:59,439
Waterfall is the oldest, and simplest, arrangement.

15
00:00:59,989 --> 00:01:04,170
The stages run in order, and each one finishes before the next begins.

16
00:01:04,720 --> 00:01:09,136
Requirements, then design, then build, then test, then release, then operate.

17
00:01:09,686 --> 00:01:13,633
It works well when requirements are clear, stable, and well understood.

18
00:01:14,183 --> 00:01:16,956
For example, a fixed contract, or strict rules.

19
00:01:17,506 --> 00:01:19,256
The weakness is late feedback.

20
00:01:19,806 --> 00:01:22,600
Users may not see working software until the end.

21
00:01:23,150 --> 00:01:25,838
And a change late in the project is expensive.

22
00:01:27,888 --> 00:01:31,899
Agile arranges the same stages in small, repeated cycles.

23
00:01:32,449 --> 00:01:35,478
Each cycle delivers a small piece of working software.

24
00:01:36,028 --> 00:01:41,233
Cycle one: browse products. Cycle two: search. Cycle three: place an order.

25
00:01:41,783 --> 00:01:45,239
After every cycle, the team shows the result, and listens.

26
00:01:45,789 --> 00:01:51,208
Agile began as a set of values, written in 2001, called the Agile Manifesto.

27
00:01:51,758 --> 00:01:56,579
It prefers collaboration, working software, and a readiness to respond to change.

28
00:01:57,129 --> 00:02:00,095
Agile is a way of thinking, not one fixed method.

29
00:02:00,645 --> 00:02:03,759
Scrum and Kanban are two popular ways to practice it.

30
00:02:05,809 --> 00:02:08,241
Scrum is a framework for doing Agile work.

31
00:02:08,791 --> 00:02:12,674
Work happens in sprints: fixed time boxes of one month or less.

32
00:02:13,224 --> 00:02:14,909
A Scrum team has three roles.

33
00:02:15,459 --> 00:02:18,488
The product owner decides what is most valuable.

34
00:02:19,038 --> 00:02:21,257
The developers build and test the increment.

35
00:02:21,807 --> 00:02:25,690
The Scrum master helps the team follow Scrum, and removes blockers.

36
00:02:26,240 --> 00:02:28,734
Each sprint has four events: planning,

37
00:02:28,734 --> 00:02:30,309
a 15-minute daily scrum,

38
00:02:30,309 --> 00:02:30,900
a review,

39
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:32,213
and a retrospective.

40
00:02:32,763 --> 00:02:36,347
The result is a usable increment, at the end of every sprint.

41
00:02:38,397 --> 00:02:40,936
Kanban is a different way to organize the work.

42
00:02:41,486 --> 00:02:44,536
Work is shown on a board, with a column for each step.

43
00:02:45,086 --> 00:02:46,452
To do, doing, and done.

44
00:02:47,002 --> 00:02:49,732
The key rule is a limit on work in progress.

45
00:02:50,282 --> 00:02:54,250
If doing is full, the team finishes something before starting anything new.

46
00:02:54,800 --> 00:02:57,638
That keeps work flowing, and shows bottlenecks early.

47
00:02:58,188 --> 00:03:02,262
Kanban has no fixed sprints, so work can arrive and finish at any time.

48
00:03:02,812 --> 00:03:05,308
It suits support and operations work well.

49
00:03:07,358 --> 00:03:09,193
DevOps goes one step further.

50
00:03:09,743 --> 00:03:11,961
Agile gets code written in small steps.

51
00:03:12,511 --> 00:03:16,799
DevOps carries that code all the way to a running system, and keeps it healthy.

52
00:03:17,349 --> 00:03:18,757
Three ideas stand out.

53
00:03:19,307 --> 00:03:23,147
Shared ownership: developers and operations work as one team.

54
00:03:23,697 --> 00:03:27,836
Automation: building, testing, and deploying are done by pipelines.

55
00:03:28,386 --> 00:03:32,119
Fast feedback: monitoring shows what users really experience.

56
00:03:32,669 --> 00:03:38,365
DevOps is not a tool, and not a job title. It is a way of working, supported by tools.

57
00:03:38,915 --> 00:03:41,305
You will learn those tools later in this bootcamp.

58
00:03:43,355 --> 00:03:45,211
Let us compare them side by side.

59
00:03:45,761 --> 00:03:50,838
Waterfall: stages in strict order. Best when requirements are clear and stable.

60
00:03:51,388 --> 00:03:56,103
Agile: small cycles with steady feedback. Best when needs are still changing.

61
00:03:56,653 --> 00:04:01,368
Scrum: sprints, roles, and events. Best when one team builds one product.

62
00:04:01,918 --> 00:04:06,120
Kanban: a board with work limits. Best for a steady flow of requests.

63
00:04:06,670 --> 00:04:09,819
DevOps: development and operations as one team,

64
00:04:09,819 --> 00:04:10,891
with automation.

65
00:04:10,891 --> 00:04:13,169
Best for keeping software running,

66
00:04:13,169 --> 00:04:14,308
and improving it.

67
00:04:14,858 --> 00:04:17,972
Notice they are not rivals. Many teams combine them.

68
00:04:20,022 --> 00:04:22,177
Here is CloudCart, built both ways.

69
00:04:22,727 --> 00:04:27,313
With Waterfall, the team spends months on requirements, design, build, and test.

70
00:04:27,863 --> 00:04:30,487
Customers see the shop for the first time at the end.

71
00:04:31,037 --> 00:04:34,664
With Agile, a small piece is ready every month, or even sooner.

72
00:04:35,214 --> 00:04:37,497
Browse first, then search, then ordering.

73
00:04:38,047 --> 00:04:41,524
Customers can react early, and the team can change direction.

74
00:04:42,074 --> 00:04:44,591
Same stages, same goal, different rhythm.

75
00:04:46,641 --> 00:04:48,646
Now match situations to methods.

76
00:04:49,196 --> 00:04:54,295
Fixed, well understood requirements, and strict sign-off? Waterfall-style planning fits.

77
00:04:54,845 --> 00:04:58,600
A new product where needs are unclear? Agile, often with Scrum.

78
00:04:59,150 --> 00:05:02,627
A team handling a steady stream of support requests? Kanban.

79
00:05:03,177 --> 00:05:08,105
Keeping a live shop healthy, with frequent, safe releases? DevOps practices.

80
00:05:08,655 --> 00:05:11,514
Real teams mix these, and adjust as they learn.

81
00:05:13,564 --> 00:05:15,420
Watch out for four common myths.

82
00:05:15,970 --> 00:05:21,730
Myth one: Agile means no planning. In fact, Agile plans all the time, in smaller steps.

83
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:27,080
Myth two: Scrum and Agile are the same. Scrum is one way to practice Agile.

84
00:05:27,630 --> 00:05:32,110
Myth three: DevOps is a tool, or a job title. It is a way of working.

85
00:05:32,660 --> 00:05:34,932
Myth four: Waterfall is always wrong.

86
00:05:34,932 --> 00:05:36,959
It can still fit work with fixed,

87
00:05:36,959 --> 00:05:38,740
well understood requirements.

88
00:05:39,290 --> 00:05:41,829
Choose by the situation, not by fashion.

89
00:05:43,879 --> 00:05:44,754
Quick check.

90
00:05:45,304 --> 00:05:47,373
How is Waterfall different from Agile?

91
00:05:47,923 --> 00:05:49,864
What are the three roles in a Scrum team?

92
00:05:50,414 --> 00:05:52,100
What is the key rule in Kanban?

93
00:05:52,650 --> 00:05:54,676
Is DevOps a tool? Explain.

94
00:05:55,226 --> 00:05:59,280
Objective covered: MC01-O03.

95
00:05:59,830 --> 00:06:01,899
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

96
